+> Plugin-only availability gate: `sc-workflow-protocol`, `sc:workflow-protocol`, `task-builder`, and `rf-task-builder` are UNAVAILABLE. With `--remediate`, surface the selected missing builder and report path, set `remediation_unavailable: true`, both remediation paths and `task_file_path` null, `remediation_accepted: false`, and `handoff_memory_key: null`. Never run the phases or substitute an executor. The source integration contract below is retained as unavailable documentation only. Python wrapper behavior requires the separately installed package, not bundled here.
+
+# Wave 6 Remediation Handoff — Plan Route (default) and task-builder Route (`--depth deep`)
+
+Loaded by Wave 6 only when `--remediate` is set AND Wave 5 produced a deviation register with at least one item whose class warrants a remediation (see default-remediation table below). Reflect itself never fixes code; this ref documents the opt-in handoff. The default chain builds a workflow tasklist (`.dev/tasks/to-do/<slug>/<slug>.md`, a managed `TASK-WF-*` package) through `sc:workflow` for the user to run with `/sc:sc-implement`; the `task-builder` → `/task` chain runs only with `--depth deep`.
+
+Source for the MDTM route: `task-builder/SKILL.md` lines 785-985 (M1-frozen BUILD_REQUEST schema, plus the API-001-M2 `EXECUTION_CONTEXT_REQUIREMENTS` extension). Merged-requirements driver: §7 row "task-builder (skill, not agent) — Wave 6", §8 cross-skill table, §10.3 Drift, §10.4 Regression.
+
+## Route selection
+
+The route is keyed on the `--depth` the user **requested**, never on the tier reached (an auto-escalation to Tier 2 does not change the route). `--remediate` is the gate on reflect, the analogue of `--fix` on troubleshoot.
+
+| Flags | Route | Builder | User-run executor |
+|-------|-------|---------|-------------------|
+| no `--remediate` (any depth, including `--depth deep` alone) | **None** | none | none |
+| `--remediate` (depth `standard`, the default, or `quick`) | **Plan route** | `sc:workflow-protocol` → package tasklist `<pkg>/<slug>.md` (its built-in advisory `--mode pre` review is surfaced) | `/sc:sc-implement <pkg>/<slug>.md` |
+| `--remediate --depth deep` | **MDTM route** | `task-builder` → MDTM task file | `/task <task-file>` |
+| headless `claude --print` + `--remediate` (reflect wrapper, `superclaude reflect run --fix`), depth `standard`/`quick` | **Plan route** (same depth rule) | `sc:workflow-protocol` → package tasklist `<pkg>/<slug>.md` | the wrapper auto-runs `/sc:sc-implement <remediation_plan_path>` |
+| headless `claude --print` + `--remediate --depth deep` | **MDTM route** | `task-builder` | the wrapper auto-runs `/task <remediation_task_path>` |
+
+- `--depth deep` alone does **not** trigger remediation: Wave 6 still requires `--remediate`.
+- `--depth quick` with `--remediate` takes the plan route; there is no new STOP for it.
+- The skill never runs `/sc:sc-implement` or `/task`, and never passes `--handoff implement` to `sc:workflow` (that would auto-run `sc:implement`). The route-specific phases below stop at a user-run execution gate.
+- Headless runs follow the same depth-keyed rule; the only differences are auto-accept and no Phase B/C user gates. See "Headless auto-accept under `--print` (FR-9)".
+- **Sanctioned nested call.** Reflect's Wave 6 does not call reflect on the plan itself. The `/sc:sc-reflect --mode pre --spec <pkg>/source.md --tasklist <pkg>/<slug>.md` call is made by `sc:workflow` in its own Wave 4 (advisory, no `--remediate`, its own output). This is the one sanctioned exception to the SKILL.md Will Not rule against reflecting on reflect's own intermediate output.
+- A `TASK-WF-*` package is an `/sc:sc-implement` package: never give it to `/task`. `sc:workflow` no longer accepts `--output`; never pass it.
+
+## Plan route (`--remediate`) — Phases A–D
+
+`sc:workflow` and `sc:implement` have no `BUILD_REQUEST`-style field, so any reflect state that must survive (Regression items, Drift decisions, `needs_human_decision`, grounding gaps) travels as a plain section in the source file handed to `sc:workflow`. The `sc:workflow` gate forbids checkbox (`- [ ]`) and numbered-list lines anywhere in the tasklist (`sc:implement` would enumerate them as tasks), so the source uses plain dash bullets and prose only. `sc:workflow` snapshots the source byte-exact as `<pkg>/source.md`, where `<pkg>` = `.dev/tasks/to-do/<slug>`.
+
+### Phase A — Build the plan
+
+1. Surface the opt-in prompt below (plan-route steps only). `yes` proceeds; anything else, including an ambiguous answer, is `no`.
+2. Write `<output-dir>/remediation-source.md` (the `sc:workflow` source), built from the report and the deviation register:
+
+   ```text
+   # Remediation: <REPORT.md title>
+
+   Goal: Remediate the Regression-class and Drift-class deviations in <abs-path-to-report.md>
+
+   ## Summary
+   <REPORT.md summary, verbatim>
+
+   ## Deviations To Remediate
+   - Regression <D-id>: <title> - <file:line> - <one-line required repair>
+   - Drift <D-id>: <title> - <file:line> - decision: <backfill spec | revert>
+
+   ## Files
+   - <files from the deviation register>
+
+   ## Acceptance
+   - <previously failing test or check per Regression item, as a plain bullet>
+   - The change is limited to the files listed under Files
+   - No new lint or type errors introduced
+
+   ## Review Feedback
+   <contents of <output-dir>/remediation-review-notes.md, flattened; omit this whole section on the first pass>
+
+   ## References
+   - Report: <abs-path>
+   - Deviation register: <abs-path>
+   - Grounding gaps: <abs-path>
+
+   ## Reflect Verdict State
+   needs_human_decision: <true | false>
+   regression_count: <n>
+   drift_count: <n>
+   grounding_gaps: <none | see References>
+   (Downstream stages may append findings but must not downgrade a Regression item or clear needs_human_decision.)
+   ```
+
+   Drift items need a per-item backfill-or-revert decision; when undecided, say in prose that deciding it is the first change the plan must make (no numbered list).
+
+   **Flatten list syntax.** Anything copied verbatim into this source (the REPORT.md summary, register rows, test names) must first be flattened: rewrite every numbered line (`1.`, `1)`) and every checkbox line (`- [ ]`, `- [x]`, `* [ ]`) as a plain `- ` bullet or a prose sentence, keeping its full text (never delete a line). A single leftover list line makes the `sc:workflow` gate fail with `E-GATE` (or makes `sc:implement` enumerate it as a task).
+3. Invoke `Skill sc:workflow-protocol` with `<output-dir>/remediation-source.md --depth standard --handoff none`. Never pass `--handoff implement`: that auto-invokes `sc:implement`, which edits product code, and the execution gate (Phase C) is always user-initiated. Do not pass `--output` (retired in `sc:workflow`; it STOPs `E-LEGACY`). The package is always created at `.dev/tasks/to-do/<slug>/`.
+4. Read `<pkg>/return-contract.yaml` (contract 1.1). `status: success` → resolve the absolute tasklist path (`<pkg>` + `plan_path`, which is package-relative `./<slug>.md`), record it as `plan_path` in `audit.log` and emit it as the `remediation_plan_path` contract field (§9.1, 1.8.0). Any other status (for example `E-GATE`) → surface it, record `plan_path=null`, do not retry automatically.
+5. `<pkg>/source.md` is the byte-exact snapshot of `remediation-source.md`, so the `## Reflect Verdict State` section is already carried there. Verify the tasklist `<slug>.md` also carries the `needs_human_decision` / `regression_count` lines; if missing, append the section to the tasklist verbatim, keeping it free of checkbox and numbered-list lines. (`sc:workflow` has no such field of its own, so reflect owns this carry.)
+
+### Phase B — Consume the built-in pre-review
+
+`sc:workflow` already runs `/sc:sc-reflect --mode pre --spec <pkg>/source.md --tasklist <pkg>/<slug>.md` as its Wave 4 (advisory, read-only). Reflect does **not** make a separate call on the plan; it consumes that result. Read `reflect_status` and `reflect_report_path` from `<pkg>/return-contract.yaml` and surface both with the tasklist path.
+
+- Findings never edit the tasklist (`sc:workflow` forbids it). To change the plan, append the recommendation to `<output-dir>/remediation-review-notes.md` (append-only; Phase A step 2 rewrites `remediation-source.md` from scratch each pass and re-includes these notes as `## Review Feedback`, so they are never lost) and re-run Phase A; that creates a NEW package.
+- `reflect_status: skipped|failed` (`reflect_report_path: null`) → say so and fall back to spawning the `sc:sc-self-review` agent on `<pkg>/<slug>.md`.
+- Coverage caveat: the tasklist is read as a free-form file; exact-ID matching (`R-`, `REQ-`, `SPEC-`, `T-` tokens) will not fire on `## Task N:` headings, so spec rows match through the Pass-2 containment rule only. Treat `coverage_undefined` as a surfaced warning, not a silent pass.
+- Interactive outcomes: no findings → proceed to Phase C. Findings or a blocker → surface them and ask "refactor the plan before running, or proceed as-is? [refactor / proceed]"; on `refactor`, re-run Phase A as above. A blocker → STOP and do not advance to Phase C until the user resolves it.
+- Headless: the built-in advisory pre-review still runs inside `sc:workflow` (extra cost), but the wrapper does not gate on its findings.
+
+### Phase C — Execution gate (always user-initiated)
+
+Record `baseline_ref = git rev-parse HEAD` in `audit.log`, then surface the path and the literal commands:
+
+```text
+Plan ready: <abs-path to .dev/tasks/to-do/<slug>/<slug>.md>
+
+To execute, run:
+  /sc:sc-implement <abs-path>
+
+The skill stops here. After /sc:sc-implement completes, resolve the package by id (it is archived to
+.dev/tasks/done/<slug>/ after a fresh FINAL; use whichever of to-do/done exists) and run the post-execution gate:
+  /sc:sc-reflect --mode post --spec <report.md> --tasklist <resolved-pkg>/<slug>.md --diff <baseline_ref> --task-log <resolved-pkg>/progress.md
+```
+
+A single ref (not `A..B`) diffs against the working tree, so `--diff <baseline_ref>` captures uncommitted `/sc:sc-implement` edits to tracked files; `git diff` omits untracked files, so `--task-log` (the package's sibling `progress.md` ledger) lets reflect see files the run created. This is non-negotiable: the skill never runs `/sc:sc-implement` — running it on the user's behalf bypasses their final review of what is about to change.
+
+### Phase D — Post-execution validation (optional, user-triggered)
+
+Only fires if the user comes back and explicitly invokes the gate. The skill itself does not poll and does not auto-resume after `/sc:sc-implement`. The surfaced command carries no `--remediate` (no loop).
+
+`OK` → emit "Validation passed — safe to commit." Issues → surface them; do not auto-fix. The user decides whether to revert, patch, or accept.
+
